course: dictionary
lesson: 6890
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «If you overtake the runner in second place, you take second place; the leader is still ahead of you.» Here overtake means to catch up with someone going the same way and get past them.
explain: Overtake can also mean to become bigger or better than someone. «India overtook China around 2023» does not mean India drove past China on a road. It means India became the country with more people.
explain: Overtake can also mean something happens to you suddenly, like a feeling. «Fear overtook him» means the fear came on him quickly and strongly. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence best.
explain: In road safety, cars overtake cyclists and must leave plenty of space. Overtaking is dangerous on bends and hills, where drivers cannot always see what is coming.
fact: «Overtake» means to catch up with someone and go past them.
fact: «Overtook» is the past tense of overtake, and «overtaken» is the form used with has or had.
fact: Overtake can mean to become bigger or better than someone, or to happen suddenly to someone, like a feeling.
fact: American English often says «pass» instead of overtake.
fact: A good check is to swap in a simple meaning, such as «went past», and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «India overtook China around 2023.» What does overtook mean here? | Stayed behind China | *Became bigger than China | Lost to China in a game | Ran away from China
exercise: Which word is the past tense of overtake? | overtakes | overtake | *overtook | overtaking
exercise: In road safety, cars must leave plenty of space when they overtake what? | buses | cars | *cyclists | roads
test: «Fear overtook him in the dark wood.» What does overtook suggest? | He ran past the fear | He was bigger than fear | *It happened to him suddenly, like a feeling | He forgot the wood
test: Where is overtaking dangerous? | On straight, flat roads | In a quiet car park | At home in the kitchen | *On bends and hills
exam: In American English, which word is often used instead of overtake? | sprint | *pass | wave | lean
